{{also|throw-off|throwoff}}
==English==

===Pronunciation===
* {{audio|en|En-au-throw off.ogg|Audio (AU)}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb|throw<,,threw,thrown> off}}

# {{lb|en|idiomatic}} To [[confuse]]; especially, to [[lose]] a [[pursue]]r.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1940| title=The Story of Mona Sheehy |author=Lord Dunsany | page=170| passage="Then," said the tinker, "maybe the thing for us to do would be to go by queer ways, by bohereens and the like of that, and for us to get of the straight road to Rathmoon, the way that no-one will find us except the tinkers, for we can't '''throw off''' them."}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1999| title=Trailsman 207: Chimney Rock Burial|author=Jon Sharpe | page=| passage="Well," he said as they reached the livery, "we could go north or '''throw them off'' our trail. That would add a day to our trip, though."}}
#: {{ux|en|I never saw her without glasses before, so it '''threw''' me '''off''' when she got contact lenses.}}
# {{lb|en|idiomatic}} To introduce [[error]]s or [[inaccuracies]]; to [[skew]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2011| title=Inside Baseball: The Best of Tom Verducci|author=Tom Verducci | page=| passage=“Varying the speeds of your pitches to '''throw off''' the timing of the hitter is much more important than it used to be,” Yankees righthander David Cone says.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2020| title=No Days Off: My Life with Type 1 Diabetes and Journey to the NHL|author=Max Domi | page=54| passage=Too much activity one day can '''throw off''' my blood sugar as much as if I am not active the next.}}
#: {{ux|en|The dirt in the apparatus '''threw off''' the results.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} Of a horse, to eject its rider.
# To [[fling]] [[away]].
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=March 11, 1882 |work=The Living Age |title=The Vistas of the Past: The Moon and the Earth|author=Richard A. Proctor|volume=152 |page=813|passage=Long before the tidal wave had again grwon sufficiently high to '''throw off''' spray, the spray last '''thrown off''' would have passed beyond its reach. }}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1910| title=The Federal Reporter - Volume 180 |author=| page=423| passage=It was conceded on the final hearing and is shown by the proof, and I find as a fact that, while this rapid revolution of the cans fresh from the solder bath would throw off superflous solder, it would frequently '''throw off''' too much at points so as to leave unsoldered places, causing leaks . }}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2018| title=Discoveries and Inventions of the Ninteenth Century |author=Robert Routledge | page=123| passage=The requirements of the traffic necessitate not only solidly contructed iron-covered snow-sheds, but massive snow-ploughs to '''throw off''' the track the deep snow which could no other way be prevented from interrupting the working of the line . }}
# To [[expel]], [[reject]], or [[renounce]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1727| title=Materia Medica |author=Edward Strother | page=196| passage=''Silver is gotten thus'', They take the Ore and dry it, then they burn it, and powder it very fine, they dilute it with Water, and  '''throw off''' the light Powder that swims at Top, by decanting gently: }}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1852| title=A Dictionary of Domestic Medicine and Household Surgery |author=Spencer Thomson | page=194| passage=Moreover, during the progress of disease, nothing assists more the powers of the constitution, which tends toward health, and to '''throw off''' the enemy, than a cheerful and hopeful mind; as the people call it, a "good spirit;"}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1914| title=Reports of Cases Decided in the Supreme Court of the State of Oregon|author=| page=| passage=They generally shut the mill down a little (when the belts are to be '''thrown off'''), and you have to take the belt in your hand and '''throw it off'''.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2011| title=The Complete Day Skipper|author=Tom Cunliffe | page=| passage=If you don't have enough crew, just '''throw off''' everything except the stern line, then bring that in as you engage gear.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2013| title=Conversation in a Train and Other Critical Writings|author=Frank Sargeson | page=| passage=Now it would be too much to say that the New Zealand novelist has to get completely out from under the shadow of these great men—nevertheless it is true that he must endeavour to '''throw off''' their influence, insofar as it may hinder him from attempting to grapple with the New Zealand scene. }}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2017| title=Colossians: A Commentary|author=David J. Landegent | page=| passage=We might expect Paul to tell the colossians to '''throw off''' the same sins that they were to necrotize, but instead he provides a new list of five sins which need to be removed.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2018| title=Tournament Bridge for Beginning Players|author=Ken Casey | page=| passage=Whenever you have a singleton and QJx, you get to '''throw off''' 2 losers, not just one. }}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2018| title=Therapy, Stand-Up, and the Gesture of Writing|author=Jonathan Wyatt | page=| passage=Reality itself, in the way of the abject, keeps returning to the stand-up comedian, who '''throws it off''' in the form of jokes.}}
# To [[split]] off.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1824| title=An Encyclopaedia of Gardening|author=John Claudius Loudon | page=968| passage=And when what is called the black sap is descending the tree, the bark taken off is black, and loses its original color; and at this time also the bark begins to '''throw off''' a scurf , more especially young bark without much cork on it ; this outer skin having less of the proper sap or juice , and being much drier when taken off , will weigh less , and consequently will not be so valuable .}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1826|month=June|work=Blackwood's Edinburgh Magazine |title=Geography of Central Africa: Denham and Clapperton's Journals|author=|volume=19|page=700|passage=The Reviewer states: "''Bellal'' told Major Denham that the KOWARA did not '''throw off''' a branch at ''Boussa'', but that a CONFLUENCE of branches took place ''at Boussa'', the one from the ''south'' and the other from the NORTHWEST."}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1941|month=March|title=Notes and News: Railway Isolation in the Pennines|journal=Railway Magazine|page=140|text=This is the branch of the Midland Division of the L.M.S.R. from Garsdale to Hawes, incidentally the only branch '''thrown off''' by the Settle and Carlisle main line in the 73 miles of its length from Settle junction to Petteril junction, Carlisle.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2022| title=Lectures and Essays|author=Thomas Henry Huxley | page=| passage=That is the case with many bulbous plants, which '''throw off''' in this way secondary bulbs, which are lodged in the ground and become developed into plants.}}
# To give forth in an [[unpremeditated]] manner.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1904| title=Notes on Track: Construction and Maintenance - Volume 2 |author=Walter Mason Camp | page=720| passage=Then when the work train comes along it will be an easy matter to '''throw off''' the required number, almost in place.}}
# To [[deduct]] from a price in order to compensate for problems.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1885| title=Journal of the Senate of the State of Ohio - Volume 81 |author=| page=182| passage=Now , wasn't it the understanding that you and Hall together were to '''throw off''' two thousand dollars from the amount, or from your interest in bid?}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1897| title=Supreme Court of the State of New York, Appellate Division Third Dept. Vol. 72 |author=| page=36| passage=Mr. Parsons came in to see me about the matter, and wanted to know what I would '''throw off''' from the mortgage. I told him I wouldn't '''throw off''' one cent, and I had no reason why I should '''throw off''' anything, that I was protected.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1915| title=The Northwestern Reporter - Volume 152 |author=| page=| passage=I wanted him to '''throw off''' $1.25 an acre for the land not plowed.}}

